1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,281
Frequency bands are the neighborhoods of the radio spectrum.

2
00:00:06,281 --> 00:00:12,202
HF, VHF, UHF — the letters you see on every scanner listing and band plan — are the

3
00:00:12,202 --> 00:00:18,052
conventional decade-wide divisions of the radio spectrum: HF is 3 to 30 megahertz,

4
00:00:18,052 --> 00:00:23,902
VHF is 30 to 300 megahertz, UHF is 300 megahertz to 3 gigahertz, with VLF, LF, and

5
00:00:23,902 --> 00:00:25,543
MF below and SHF above.

6
00:00:25,543 --> 00:00:31,108
Each decade behaves so differently in propagation, antenna size, and available

7
00:00:31,108 --> 00:00:35,032
bandwidth that engineers treat it as a distinct regime.

8
00:00:35,032 --> 00:00:40,454
Learn the neighborhoods and you know what to expect before you ever tune in.

9
00:00:40,904 --> 00:00:42,560
A city works the same way.

10
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:47,784
Districts are drawn on a map by convention, but each one genuinely feels different

11
00:00:47,784 --> 00:00:52,816
— how you get around, how big the buildings are, what business gets done there.

12
00:00:52,816 --> 00:00:57,849
The band boundaries are round numbers, not physics walls, yet cross one and the

13
00:00:57,849 --> 00:01:00,078
rules of the road really do change.

14
00:01:00,528 --> 00:01:02,949
Why does each decade feel different?

15
00:01:02,949 --> 00:01:03,689
Wavelength.

16
00:01:03,689 --> 00:01:09,069
Because wavelength is c over frequency, HF wavelengths are tens of metres, VHF a

17
00:01:09,069 --> 00:01:14,650
few metres, UHF centimetres to a metre — and that directly sets antenna size, since

18
00:01:14,650 --> 00:01:18,820
an efficient antenna is a meaningful fraction of a wavelength.

19
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:22,317
But the biggest difference is how each band travels.

20
00:01:22,317 --> 00:01:27,831
HF refracts off the ionosphere: that sky-wave "skip" can span continents on modest

21
00:01:27,831 --> 00:01:33,211
power, with conditions swinging by the sun, the time of day, and the eleven-year

22
00:01:33,211 --> 00:01:34,018
solar cycle.

23
00:01:34,018 --> 00:01:39,196
VHF and UHF normally punch straight through the ionosphere and travel in near

24
00:01:39,196 --> 00:01:43,701
straight lines — range set by the radio horizon and antenna height.

25
00:01:43,701 --> 00:01:49,283
That predictability is exactly why land-mobile, aviation, and public-safety systems

26
00:01:49,283 --> 00:01:54,864
live there, though occasional tropospheric ducting and sporadic-E can fling VHF far

27
00:01:54,864 --> 00:01:56,142
beyond the horizon.

28
00:01:56,142 --> 00:02:01,724
SHF and above go strongly line-of-sight, offer huge bandwidth for radar, satellite,

29
00:02:01,724 --> 00:02:04,346
and 5G — and start to suffer rain fade.

30
00:02:04,796 --> 00:02:09,756
There's a running trade as you climb: higher frequencies carry more path loss for a

31
00:02:09,756 --> 00:02:14,536
given distance and antenna, and penetrate buildings less — but they buy the wide

32
00:02:14,536 --> 00:02:16,986
bandwidth high-rate digital systems need.

33
00:02:16,986 --> 00:02:20,213
Range at the bottom of the ruler, capacity at the top.

34
00:02:20,213 --> 00:02:24,934
Every wireless system is parked where its trade balances — and once you see the

35
00:02:24,934 --> 00:02:29,714
ruler that way, an unfamiliar frequency starts telling you a story before you've

36
00:02:29,714 --> 00:02:31,387
heard a single transmission.

37
00:02:31,837 --> 00:02:35,799
For a scanner operator, the map gets specific.

38
00:02:35,799 --> 00:02:42,775
Regulators — coordinated globally by the ITU, administered nationally by the FCC,

39
00:02:42,775 --> 00:02:49,836
Ofcom, and others — publish band plans carving each band into slices for services.

40
00:02:49,836 --> 00:02:56,554
Three slices to memorize: VHF high band, 136 to 174 megahertz — marine, nearby

41
00:02:56,554 --> 00:03:01,290
aviation voice, older public-safety and business radio.

42
00:03:01,290 --> 00:03:07,147
UHF, 400 to 520 — business, public safety, and many trunked systems.

43
00:03:07,147 --> 00:03:14,208
And 700/800 megahertz — modern public-safety trunked radio, P25, in North America.

44
00:03:14,658 --> 00:03:19,856
This is why GopherTrunk ships band presets: pick the target band and the scanner

45
00:03:19,856 --> 00:03:23,040
starts looking where that traffic actually lives.

46
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:28,108
But the band decides more than the tuning — it determines the antenna type and

47
00:03:28,108 --> 00:03:33,307
size, the expected path loss, the feedline, whether your SDR's range even covers

48
00:03:33,307 --> 00:03:36,231
it, and which protocols are likely to appear.

49
00:03:36,231 --> 00:03:40,844
Matching the band is the first hardware decision, ahead of any software

50
00:03:40,844 --> 00:03:41,753
configuration.

51
00:03:42,203 --> 00:03:47,722
So: the bands are decade-wide neighborhoods, each with its own propagation and

52
00:03:47,722 --> 00:03:53,098
antenna scale — HF skips off the sky, VHF and UHF run line-of-sight, and the

53
00:03:53,098 --> 00:03:57,555
trunked traffic you're after lives in three UHF-and-VHF slices.

54
00:03:57,555 --> 00:03:59,465
Full write-up linked below.

